this is Lucy James speaking for  Alessandra filari the opening hours of  Resident Evil 3 are incredibly effective  at putting you on edge  placing the volatile and intense  conflict between protagonist Jill  Valentine and the unrelenting force of  nature  the Nemesis front and center the remake  of the 1999 game presents some strong  survival horror moments that show off  the best of what the series can offer  but after that solid start it becomes  clear that this revisit to a bygone era  not only loses track of the type of  horror game that Resident Evil once was  but also loses sight of what made the  original so memorable  re3 is very much a companion piece to  resident evil' to serving as a  simultaneous prequel and sequel that  caps off the saga in Raccoon City the  remake of Resident Evil three interprets  the classic survival horror through a  modern lens redesigning locations and  altering key events to fit a  significantly revised story  it doesn't deviate too much from the  formula set by its predecessor but it  does have a more pronounced focus on  action horror returning protagonist Jill  Valentine is a capable survivor and  she's well suited to handle the undead  and other bioweapon monstrosities like  nemesis that roam the city along with  dodging attacks she can also pull off  slow-mo evasive roles that open a clean  shot at the enemy's weak point which can  be especially satisfying to pull off  during an intense encounter while this  ultimately gives you a greater feeling  of control in the field it's not enough  to the point where it makes these  encounters with the ravenous undead or  nemesis easy  ari 3s introduction is a strong one  conveying a creeping sense of paranoia  and dread that's synonymous with the  series and Jill Valentine once again  proves herself to be a confident  protagonist to take everything head-on  you'll eventually cross paths with  supporting characters like mercenary  Carlos La Vie era the game's second  playable character along with some other  unsavory individuals looking to take  advantage of the chaos for their own  ends the key players also get their own  big moments throughout the plot which  will earned and are fun to see play out  but disappointingly re3 story reaches  its conclusion after a brisk six hour  campaign which is made worse by a  lackluster finish that left me wanting  while this is comparable to the 1999  game the shrinking scope of the remix  campaign after its great opening makes  the short run all the more clear  still re3 possesses an impeccable level  of detail for the locations and action  during its brief campaign one of my  favorite areas of the game is set in  downtown Raccoon City which shows off  the best the remake has to offer as an  expansive map it not only presents  diverse locations packed with plenty of  undead it also displays the dynamic  nature of the game's true star the  Nemesis during these moments the game's  antagonist actively stalks and follows  you into places you want thought were  safe bending the unspoken rules of  engagement in survival horror to  establish its presence further nemesis  makes the tyrant from RE to come across  as a fedora wearing goon and even when  you find yourself in a safe space you  can catch a glimpse at the villain  waiting outside for you to leave which  still stands as some of the most  unnerving moments in the game however as  the game goes on it becomes clear that  Resident Evil three struggles to  maintain a good pace just when you're  getting accustomed to a location a story  beat will occur that will bring you to  the next area preventing your return  puzzles are also infrequent which puts  more of its focus on exploration and  combat that becomes a bit stale towards  the end of the game there are some  moments where the game strengths for  action and horror shine but generally  its attempts to balance both can come  across as hollow and at its worst they  can be jarring this is especially true  with the many elaborate set-piece  encounters where you control Jill as she  navigates uncharted liked sequences they  often result in tonal whiplash as you  typically transition back into the  slower survival horror pacing right  after this is the most evident in the  portrayal of the game's primary  antagonist nemesis is at its best when  stalking you in the streets but these  sections while terrifyingly engaging are  fleeting  in actuality Nemesis only actively hunts  you during the opening section in  downtown Raccoon City with other  encounters thereafter tied to scripted  sequences and boss fights the nemesis is  undoubtedly one of the most iconic  villains of the series and it's  frustrating to see how the remake locks  away one of the game's biggest assets  behind uninteresting and counters after  you finished the campaign Resident Evil  three opens up a bonus shop in the main  menu that lets you purchase new costumes  and extra items with credits gained  from completing challenges while these  can be fun for a revisit there isn't  much else in the way of bonus content to  keep you going also included with the  remake is resident evil resistance an  asymmetrical online mode pitting for  survivors against a lone player in the  role of the overseeing antagonist in  essence it's a more accessible take on  the franchise's previous experiment with  online horror Resident Evil Outbreak  with some echoes of other asymmetrical  games like dead by daylight or left for  dead playing as the survivors feels  closer to traditional Resident Evil  where resources are scarce and the  stakes become increasingly more  significant using each survivors skills  effectively can come in clutch during  into fight saving you and your team from  the masterminds tactics resistance has  interesting ideas when it comes to  bringing the series survival horror game  play online and does an adequate job of  getting you up to speed with solid  tutorials yet the overall execution of  the games mechanics often comes off as  clunky and undercooked the controls for  the survivors feel unresponsive  especially when trying to get a clean  hit on fast-moving targets resistance  also lacks a personality of its own  while each survivor has a backstory they  mostly pull on horror movie cliches and  archetypes and they rarely speak during  the actual game which can make it hard  to connect with them the most fun to be  had in resistance is playing as the  mastermind where you can see the  survival horror game play play out from  the other side some of the most  satisfying moments come from whittling  down the defences of the survivors and  snatching away their last vestiges of  hope as they attempt their final escape  at its best it's a clever real-time  strategy twist on the survival horror  formula but generally the games already  clunky gameplay gets in the way  resistance didn't hold my attention long  and while Resident Evil 3 is the  stronger of the two  it's still disappointing looking back on  my first playthrough of the re 3 remake  it was clear that the game reached its  peak early and slowly lost momentum  going forward when looking at the  remakes shortcomings it's difficult not  to think back to the original game while  this won't mean much for newcomers it is  still apparent that the shrinking scale  of the game diminishes the impact of its  plot and events as a remake Resident  Evil three not only falls short of  honoring its source but it also doesn't  quite stick the landing  when offering a solid standalone horror  experience even without taking into  account the original game or even its  predecessor our e3 struggles to keep up  with its pace amid a clashing of  elements from survival horror and  standard action it actively tries to  have both in conflicting ways and  doesn't succeed as well as it could have  with either while it has a strong start  and gives its principal villain some  great moments this truncated retelling  of the concluding game from the original  Resident Evil trilogy doesn't do a  proper justice
 

